# Erdős Problem #99 — Target A contracted-tube sector bound

**Date:** 2026-09-04  
**Branch:** `research/target-c-c97-caterpillar-fan-capacity-2026-09-04`  
**Run type:** exact strengthening of Section 174 using the contracted Schur gammoid  
**Target:** locked three-channel sector branch  
**Status:** LEVEL I STILL OPEN

## 1. Physical basis closure is unnecessary after matching contraction

Section 174 first deletes the geometric shield

`W_Sigma^0=V(Sigma) sqcup T_Sigma`

and then physically closes it under the basis matching `mu_0:B->Z`. The closure is useful in the uncontracted alternating graph, but Section 113 permits a smaller construction.

Contract every matched edge

`mu_0(b)->b`, `b in B`,

to one internal gammoid vertex. As proved in `target_a_basis_pair_shield_contraction_2026-09-04.md`, this preserves the Schur linkage matroid.

Define the set of shielded matched pairs

`P_Sigma={b in B : b in V(Sigma) or mu_0(b) in T_Sigma}`.

Also shield

`C_V=C^partial cap V(Sigma)`

and

`O_T=O cap T_Sigma`.

In the contracted directed gammoid let

`Wbar_Sigma=P_Sigma sqcup C_V sqcup O_T`.

A symbol `b in P_Sigma` denotes the contracted pair `{b,mu_0(b)}`.

## 2. Every unshielded matched pair is sector-local

Take `b notin P_Sigma` and put `z=mu_0(b)`. Then

`b notin V(Sigma)`, `z notin T_Sigma`.

The matching edge `bz` is a response edge. Lemma 174.2 says every response edge whose right endpoint is outside `T_Sigma` avoids `|Sigma|`. Since `b` is not a contact vertex on `|Sigma|`, the two endpoints lie in one connected component of

`K^*\|Sigma|`.

Thus every surviving contracted basis pair belongs to exactly one geometric sector. No physical addition of `z` when `b` is shielded, or of `b` when `z` is shielded, is needed: that whole contracted pair is simply one shield vertex.

The same argument applies to every unshielded response edge in a shield-avoiding Schur path. Hence the entire surviving contracted path stays in one sector until its final exterior sink.

## 3. Exact contracted sector rank bound

For each sector `D_i`, let

`C_i^partial=(C^partial\V(Sigma)) cap D_i`,

and let `O_i` be the exterior sinks in `O\T_Sigma` having a surviving response neighbor in `D_i`. Lemma 174.2 makes the `O_i` pairwise disjoint.

### Theorem CT1 — contracted-tube Schur rank

`rank S <= |P_Sigma|+|C_V|+|O_T| + sum_i min(|C_i^partial|,|O_i|)`.

### Proof

Take a maximum family of vertex-disjoint directed Schur paths in the contracted gammoid. At most one path can meet each contracted shield vertex, so at most

`|P_Sigma|+|C_V|+|O_T|`

paths meet `Wbar_Sigma`.

Delete those paths. Every remaining path starts in one `C_i^partial`, stays in sector `D_i` by the preceding sector-locality argument, and ends at a distinct sink in the disjoint set `O_i`. Thus sector `i` contributes at most the displayed minimum. QED.

Subtracting from

`|C^partial|=|C_V|+sum_i |C_i^partial|`

gives:

### Corollary CT2 — contracted-tube sector nullity

`zeta >= sum_i max(0,|C_i^partial|-|O_i|) - |P_Sigma|-|O_T|`.

This is strictly stronger than the physical basis-closed estimate whenever basis closure adds a partner as a second physical vertex.

## 4. Explicit shield-capacity constant

The matched-pair shield obeys the union bound

`|P_Sigma| <= |B cap V(Sigma)| + |Z cap T_Sigma|`.

Therefore

`|P_Sigma|+|O_T|`

`<= |B cap V(Sigma)| + |T_Sigma|`

`<= |V(Sigma)|+|T_Sigma|`.

The nine-site tube theorem gives

`|T_Sigma|<=9|E(Sigma)|`.

Hence:

### Corollary CT3 — ten-per-vertex locked-fan penalty

For an arbitrary finite contact shield,

`|P_Sigma|+|O_T| <= |V(Sigma)|+9|E(Sigma)|`.

For the connected unicyclic locked three-channel fan of Section 172,

`|E(Sigma)|=|V(Sigma)|`,

so

`|P_Sigma|+|O_T| <=10|V(Sigma)|`.

Accordingly a sufficient locked-branch theorem is now

`sum_i max(0,|C_i^partial|-|O_i|) >= s+10|V(Sigma)|`.

The exact criterion is stronger:

`sum_i max(0,|C_i^partial|-|O_i|) >= s+|P_Sigma|+|O_T|`.

The earlier promoted physical-shield constant was `24|V(Sigma)|`; the nine-site improvement plus matching contraction reduces the explicit path-capacity loss to `10|V(Sigma)|`.

## 5. Basis choice remains available

The quantity

`zeta=d_sc-d_int`

is independent of the chosen `Z`-basis. Therefore, after `Sigma` is fixed, one may choose the `Z`-perfect basis matching `mu_0` to minimize `|P_Sigma|`. This is a finite minimum-cost matching problem in which a matched pair costs one exactly when its left endpoint lies in `V(Sigma)` or its right endpoint lies in `T_Sigma cap Z`.

A further exact improvement would identify that minimum cost with a local Hall/cut quantity and bound it from the stress-fan geometry.
